      "latency_ms": 2201.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning each molecule of water contains two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and its unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, found in oceans, rivers, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F), found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates, found in the air as humidity and in clouds.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Unique (Special Properties)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make life possible:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands*. This makes ice less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze solid from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\n*   **Biological Necessity:** Life as we know it cannot exist without water. The human body is made up of about 60% water. It cushions our brains, regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, and flushes out waste"
